<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Panama's Wildlife – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Panama's Wildlife</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> The Daily Habits of Animals</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify specific information in descriptions and narratives. Interactive: Engage in discussions about personal and school experiences based on texts heard.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify details from spoken narratives about animal habitats (e.g., "Which animal is faster, the jaguar or the deer?" "The deer is faster."). Can listen to descriptions of wildlife and respond to questions (e.g., "What animals live in Panama?"). Can listen to descriptions of personal and school experiences involving animals and engage in discussions by sharing opinions and asking follow-up questions (e.g., "During our school trip, we saw a sloth hanging from a tree." "What did you see during the trip?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of 3-4 iconic Panamanian animals (e.g., sloth, jaguar, harpy eagle, frog) on the board or projector. The teacher plays short, distinctive animal sounds (e.g., a jaguar's roar, a frog's croak, a bird's screech). Students listen and identify the animal. The teacher asks, "What animals live in Panama?" and "What do you know about these animals?" to activate prior knowledge about wildlife and habitats.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of a sloth and says, "This is a sloth. Sloths live in the jungle. They move slowly." The teacher explicitly models listening skills by saying, "Listen carefully for where the animal lives and how it moves." The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary like "sloth" and "jungle," practicing long vowel sounds (e.g., /sneɪk/ "snake" - teacher pronounces and students repeat, though "snake" is not core vocabulary for this lesson, it's an example of a long vowel sound; teacher focuses on /iːg/ "eagle" for long vowel and /frɒg/ "frog" for short vowel). The teacher also models syllable division for "wildlife" ("wild-life") by clapping or finger-snapping for each syllable.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do sloths move quickly or slowly?" (Checks understanding of 'slowly' and 'habit').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where does a jaguar live? In the city or in the forest?" (Checks understanding of 'habitat' and 'forest').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Panama's Wildlife."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio narrative (approx. 1-1.5 minutes) describing 2-3 Panamanian animals (e.g., jaguar, harpy eagle, sloth) and their daily habits and habitats. The audio uses present simple, "there is/there are," and basic adjectives. Example audio segment: "Welcome to Panama's wildlife! There are many diverse species here. For example, the jaguar is a large wild cat. Jaguars live in the forest and hunt prey at night. They are nocturnal predators. The harpy eagle is another powerful bird. It lives in the jungle and is stronger than most other birds. Sloths are very different. They move slowly and eat leaves."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identify specific information in descriptions and narratives (Listening: Receptive).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> The teacher plays the audio once. Students listen and then the teacher asks, "What animals did you hear about?" (Elicit: jaguar, harpy eagle, sloth).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (True/False):</b> The teacher provides 3-4 True/False statements on the board or a handout. Students listen again and mark T or F.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"Jaguars are nocturnal." (T)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"Sloths move quickly." (F)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"Harpy eagles live in the desert." (F)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third listening (Information Matching):</b> The teacher displays a simple table with two columns: "Animal" and "Habitat/Habit." Students listen a third time and match the animal to its habitat or a key daily habit. (e.g., Jaguar -> forest / hunt; Sloth -> jungle / moves slowly).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice listening for and identifying specific grammar (comparative and superlative adjectives, there is/there are, present simple) and vocabulary (habitat, wildlife, jaguar, forest, predator, prey, species, adaptation, jungle, biodiversity, protect, live, grow, hunt, survive, adapt, migrate, observe, conserve, endangered, nocturnal, wild, diverse, camouflaged, resilient). Focus on pronunciation of long/short vowels (eagle, frog) and syllable division (wildlife).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Fill-in-the-Blanks (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides a handout with sentences containing gaps. The teacher reads descriptions of animals using target grammar and vocabulary. Students listen and fill in the blanks.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"The __________ (jaguar) is a nocturnal __________ (predator)."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"__________ (There are) many __________ (diverse) __________ (species) in the Panamanian __________ (jungle)."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"Sloths __________ (live) in the trees and __________ (eat) leaves."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Compare (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher says a characteristic of one animal. Students listen and then, in pairs, say a comparative sentence using another animal's characteristic.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "The sloth is slow."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Student A (listens): "The jaguar is <b>faster</b> than the sloth." (Pragmatic: Using comparatives)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "The deer is fast."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Student B (listens): "The peregrine falcon is the <b>fastest</b> bird in Panama."</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher ensures students practice the pronunciation of words like `faster`, `stronger`, `fastest`, focusing on accurate articulation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Descriptions (Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student receives a picture of a different Panamanian animal (e.g., toucan, tapir). Student A describes their animal's habitat and daily habits (e.g., "This toucan lives in the forest. It eats fruit."). Student B listens carefully and draws the animal based on the description. Then they switch roles. After drawing, they compare their drawing to the original picture to check accuracy of listening comprehension and description. They can discuss, "The eagle is larger than the parrot." (Pragmatic: Discussing habitats and using comparatives).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Panama's Wildlife."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Animal Quiz Show - Listen and Guess!"</b> Students work in small groups (3-4). Each group prepares 2-3 short, distinct audio descriptions (pre-recorded by teacher, or read aloud by students from a prepared script) of Panamanian animals, focusing on their daily habits, habitats, and characteristics using comparative adjectives. They ensure to use vocabulary like `endangered`, `wild`, `adapt`, `survive`. One group acts as the "quizmaster," reading their descriptions aloud. The other groups listen carefully to identify the animal. After guessing, the listeners must ask one follow-up question using the target grammar or vocabulary. (E.g., "Is that animal faster than a deer?" "Does it live in the forest or the jungle?"). This facilitates group discussions about environmental topics and following instructions in collaborative activities (Sociolinguistic).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students are actively listening to detailed descriptions of Panama's wildlife and their daily habits, using all the target language elements to participate in a communicative game. This task encourages both receptive listening and interactive speaking based on the information heard.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in listening for details, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion by accurately identifying animals and asking relevant follow-up questions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Teacher Observation Checklist.</b> The teacher uses a checklist to observe students' listening comprehension during the "Animal Quiz Show" and their ability to ask follow-up questions. Criteria include: identifying animals correctly, asking relevant questions, and using target vocabulary/grammar.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: "Listen and Respond."</b> The teacher plays a final, short (30-45 sec) audio description of a new Panamanian animal (e.g., "The ocelot is a wild cat that lives in the jungle. It is smaller than a jaguar but faster than a sloth. It hunts at night."). Students listen and then answer two comprehension questions on a small slip of paper or verbally:
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What animal did you hear about?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is it faster than a sloth?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying specific information from the audio.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of response.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., jaguar, sloth, jungle, faster).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active listening and collaborative participation in group activities (Sociolinguistic).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete one of the following sentences on an exit slip:</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I learned that the __________ is __________ than the __________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it easy/difficult to understand __________ because __________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting daily habit of an animal you heard about today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What vocabulary word will you try to use when talking about animals this week?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The listening activities were effective in introducing new vocabulary and grammar in context, and students were engaged in identifying specific information. The comparative adjectives proved slightly challenging for some when forming spontaneous sentences, so more focused practice might be needed. The pronunciation of long/short vowels was well received. Adjustments for the next lesson could include incorporating more visual aids during audio tasks to support comprehension. The "Animal Quiz Show" fostered good interaction and communicative use of language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will encounter a reading text about Panamanian wildlife, using the same core vocabulary and grammatical structures introduced in this listening lesson. This will reinforce their understanding and allow them to see the language in a different modality.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>